    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Final full-deck review (facts and language)"@en ;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Scope: All 42 cards: reign years against a live Wikidata P39 query, Swedish names and house names against Wikidata sv labels and sv.wikipedia titles, notes, language tags, description and study direction; the count stated in round 5's method fix."@en ,
                                                   "Outcome: No factual or language errors; the deck was judged ready to release. One optional Swedish style suggestion (consistent wording of the two abdication notes) was applied."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q18810062>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata: the holders of the offices Monarch of England (Q18810062), Monarch of Great Britain (Q110324075), monarch of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland (Q111722535) and Monarch of the United Kingdom (Q9134365), their position-held statements (P39) with start and end times and qualifiers, their royal houses (P53), their English and Swedish labels, aliases and descriptions, and the items for the houses and the events named in the notes" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing — \"All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.\""@en .

<https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica>
    dcterms:title "Encyclopædia Britannica, 11th edition (1910–1911), as transcribed on Wikisource: the articles \"William I., King of England\", \"William II., King of England\", \"Stephen, King of England\", \"Henry II. of England\", \"Richard I.\", \"John of England\", \"Edward II.\", \"Richard II.\", \"Henry VI. of England\", \"Edward V.\", \"Richard III.\", \"Grey, Lady Jane\", \"James I. of Great Britain and Ireland\", \"Charles II. (King of England)\", \"James II. of Great Britain and Ireland\", \"William III., King of England\" and \"George IV.\"" ;
    dcterms:creator "Encyclopædia Britannica, 11th edition (article authors including Henry William Carless Davis and Charles Lethbridge Kingsford); transcribed by Wikisource contributors" ;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica (revision 15967975), licence notice: \"This work is in the public domain in the United States because it was published before January 1, 1931. This work may be in the public domain in countries and areas with longer native copyright terms that apply the rule of the shorter term to foreign works.\" The site footer's CC BY-SA licence covers Wikisource's own text, not the transcribed 1911 work. Only facts were taken from it, restated in the compiler's words; no text was copied into the deck."@en ;
    dcterms:title "Encyclopædia Britannica, 11th edition (1911), as transcribed on Wikisource" ;
    dcterms:creator "Hugh Chisholm (editor) and the Encyclopædia Britannica's contributors; transcription by Wikisource contributors" ;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica — licence notice on the page: \"This work is in the public domain in the United States because it was published before January 1, 1931. This work may be in the public domain in countries and areas with longer native copyright terms that apply the rule of the shorter term to foreign works.\" Published 1910–1911. Outside the US: the articles used are either unsigned (Poseidon, Ares, Hestia, Dionysus, Pluto, Cupid, Eros (god), Liber and Libera, and the anonymous part of Proserpine), so anonymous works published over 70 years ago, or by contributors who died more than 70 years ago (Zeus: Lewis Richard Farnell, d. 1934; Hera, Hephaestus, Hermes: Edward Ernest Sikes, d. 1940; Demeter, Athena, Apollo, Artemis, Aphrodite: John Henry Freese, d. 1930; Proserpine: James George Frazer, d. 1941; Fortuna: William Warde Fowler, d. 1921; signatures from the Wikisource headers and the articles' initials, death dates from Wikidata, query 15), and only facts, no text, were taken from them."@en ;
    dcterms:title "Encyclopædia Britannica, 11th edition (1911): the articles on the Swedish kings (Gustavus I. Eriksson to Gustavus V.) and \"Sweden\" (History), as transcribed on Wikisource" ;
    dcterms:creator "Robert Nisbet Bain (the articles on the kings from Gustavus I. to Charles XV., except Oscar I.); Oscar I., Oscar II. and Gustavus V. unsigned; \"Sweden\" by Frederick Beck, Robert Nisbet Bain, Oskar Dumrath, Osbert Howarth and Edmund Gosse; transcribed by Wikisource contributors" ;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica — licence box: \"This work is in the public domain in the United States because it was published before January 1, 1931.\" Most of the articles on the individual kings name Robert Nisbet Bain (1854–1909) as contributor in their Wikisource header (e.g. https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica/Gustavus_III.?action=raw: \"|contributor = Robert Nisbet Bain\"), so they are out of copyright under life-plus-70 terms as well; \"Oscar I.\", \"Oscar II.\" and \"Gustavus V.\" have no contributor field (unsigned), and as anonymous works published in 1910–1911 they are out of copyright under publication-based terms too. Only facts were taken, in the compiler's own words."@en .

<https://www.royal.uk/kings-and-queens-1066>
    dcterms:title "The Royal Family (royal.uk): Kings and Queens from 1066, the pages on each monarch and royal house, the Interregnum, accession, and the Court Circular" ;
